  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note-injection",
      "content": "Indirect prompt injection: both the public help-center docs and the user-submitted support tickets are attacker-influenceable. They are ingested, embedded, and indexed with no guardrail or content sanitization, then retrieved into the model prompt. A malicious instruction planted in a support ticket can hijack the assistant when that ticket is later retrieved as 'context'.",
      "category": "finding-response",
      "author": "Security Architect",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "linkedNodeId": "node-tickets"
    },
    {
      "id": "note-design",
      "content": "This is an otherwise reasonable design - TLS everywhere, authentication, RBAC on data stores, centralized logging, output post-processing. The lesson: transport and access controls do not stop indirect prompt injection. The missing control is content sanitization / a guardrail on the ingestion-to-model path.",
      "category": "decision",
      "author": "Security Architect",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "linkedNodeId": "node-ingest"
    },
    {
      "id": "note-tool",
      "content": "The Order Lookup Tool reaches confidential PII in the CRM and has no approval flow, RBAC, or logging. Combined with the injection path, a hijacked assistant could be steered to call this tool - an exfiltration chain worth modeling.",
      "category": "assumption",
      "author": "Security Architect",
      "createdAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-05-01T00:00:00.000Z",
      "linkedNodeId": "node-ordertool"
    }
  ],
  "metadata": {
    "samplePurpose": "Educational: a realistic customer-support RAG architecture that is well-built on transport/access controls but vulnerable to indirect prompt injection because untrusted ingested content reaches the model with no guardrail. Demonstrates the multi-hop indirect-injection attack path plus related agent-tool exfiltration risks."
  }
}
